;;; The **M1 alive-set invariant**, broken on purpose — M1e S1e.3.3, `ST-M1`;
;;; and the program on which breaking it cost a model, M1e2's **W4**, fixed by
;;; S1e2.2.4 (2026-11-04).
;;;
;;; ── The premise this tested ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `docs/kernel/inference/README.md` § M1 invariant, as M1 wrote it:
;;;
;;;   1. No new objects. Rules don't `:assert` facts whose args introduce
;;;      names that weren't already in the ontology / facts.
;;;   … Under these clauses, every admissible hypothesis is enumerable from
;;;   the current KB state; deeper branches **eliminate** candidates, never
;;;   extend the space.
;;;
;;; The blind enumerator built its candidate objects out of `kb.names()`,
;;; which is a function of the KB's **facts**. So a rule asserting a constant
;;; no fact mentions grew that pool only inside a fork, while the candidates
;;; were taken at *root*, where the name did not exist. The extended
;;; candidates were unreachable and nothing said so.
;;;
;;; ── What this program does ─────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;;   objects at root : A, B          (from `(p A B)`), and Z, which `spawn` names
;;;
;;;   `spawn` turns any committed `q` into `(p ?b Z)` — and **Z is a name no
;;;           fact has**: only the rule names it.
;;;   `no-a` / `no-b` refute every `q` that points at A or at B.
;;;
;;; `{(q A Z), (q B Z)}` saturates to a consistent, quiescent state, and it is
;;; the maximal one: R is ∅, `{(q A Z)}`, `{(q B Z)}` and both.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ─────────────
;;;
;;; Without `Z` among the objects, the candidates were `(q A B)` and
;;; `(q B A)`, both refuted in one firing. The walk answered
;;; `k = 0, exhausted = true`, *No solution — the constraints are
;;; contradictory*, with the file's lookahead off; that refutation was M1e2's
;;; W1 at layer 0 as much as this case (`wrong-answers/fresh-name-nospawn.ein`
;;; is this file without `spawn`, and refuted the same way). Since M1e2
;;; S1e2.1a.5 the answer is a region, and R was `{∅}`: W4 in isolation, which
;;; `wrong-answers/fresh-name-default.ein` is this file's program banked as.
;;;
;;; The user ruled on 2026-10-16 that `Z` is not new (Q-M1e.21 (b)): *"rules
;;; are part of root, so rules can't assert new names"*. A constant a rule
;;; names is a name of the program, and since S1e2.2.4 the blind enumerator's
;;; objects are seeded with every constant a rule's `:assert` names
;;; (`hypgen::candidate_objects`, from `invariant::rule_constants`). H is the
;;; six `q` candidates over A, B and Z, and R is the four states above —
;;; `examples/ein-bugs/alive-set-fresh-name-declared.ein`'s, which names `Z`
;;; in a fact. `ein-infer/tests/alive_invariant.rs` holds the pair to one R,
;;; and `corpus/wrong_answers.toml`'s `W4/alive-set-fresh-name` holds the run.
;;;
;;; Deliberately **no `(absent …)` anywhere.** The obvious encoding of "Z must
;;; be reached" is a NAF guard, and it works — but it would also give this
;;; file the shape of M1e [Q-M1e.9](../../docs/history/m1e_review_processing/open_questions.md),
;;; where `dead` is not upward-closed under `absent`, and a reader could not
;;; then tell which of the two mechanisms cost the answer. Two positive
;;; refutation rules confound nothing.
;;;
;;; ── The control ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; [`alive-set-fresh-name-declared.ein`](alive-set-fresh-name-declared.ein)
;;; is this file plus **one fact**, `(seen Z)`, which changes no rule and no
;;; constraint and does nothing but put `Z` in a fact at root. Until S1e2.2.4
;;; that one fact was the whole difference between the model and none; since
;;; then the two files describe one R.
;;;
;;; `:expect` states R. It stated `(region (model))`, ∅ alone, until the seed.

;; Its `(config :enable-pre-branch-lookahead false)` line went at M1e2 S1e2.1c.5 (2026-11-03),
;; when the user retired the flag: no route reads it, and a program that sets
;; it is refused at load.

(relation p T T)
(relation q T T)

(p A B)

(rule spawn ()
  :match  (q ?a ?b)
  :assert (p ?b Z)
  :why    "committing to (q {?a} {?b}) names Z, which no fact does")

(rule no-a ()
  :match  (q ?x A)
  :assert (false)
  :why    "nothing may reach A")

(rule no-b ()
  :match  (q ?x B)
  :assert (false)
  :why    "nothing may reach B")

(solve
  :no-hypothesis (p)
  ;; The model the seed makes reachable: `q` is named, so this closes `q`.
  :expect (region
            ;; state 1 of 4 — ∅, root itself · minimal
            (model)
            ;; state 2 of 4 — 1 candidate · from {(q A Z)}
            (model (q A Z))
            ;; state 3 of 4 — 1 candidate · from {(q B Z)}
            (model (q B Z))
            ;; state 4 of 4 — 2 candidates · maximal · from {(q A Z), (q B Z)}
            (model (q A Z) (q B Z))))

(query
  :goal   (q ?x ?y))
